In an effort to gather specific information relating to present day interaction between base municipalities and their suburban areas, the Committee conducted a comprehensive 10 study of 18 "model" cities. As stated by the Committee, the dual purpose of the study was to demonstrate (1) that data could be developed to determine the extent, if any, to which there is interaction and dependency between a base municipality and the new area of expansion proposed in the Interim Report, and (2) that the present population-mileage formula is adequate to determine the scope of commercial zones and terminal areas. The study utilized the following variables:
a) The percentage of the base municipality labor force which looks to the newly created area for work;
b) The percentage of new area work force which is drawn from the base municipality;
c) The percentage of raw materials and supplies which are drawn from the base municipality for the new area;
d) The percentage of sales in the new area which go to the base municipality;
e) The amount of land in the new area which is zoned for commercial and industrial use;
f) The amount of land in the new area which is developed for commercial and industrial use;
g) The new investment in the new area;
h) The amount of business which has relocated from the base to the new area over the last 10 years;
i) Interdependence of governmental functions;
j) Interdependence for utilities; and
